e leur langue ancestrale, mais le westron est largement employé dans la vie quotidienne et dans l’administration. Beaucoup parlent également le sindarin, tandis que les plus instruits possèdent quelques connaissances du quenya, surtout dans les domaines historiques, cérémoniels et savants.</p>\n<h2>Guerre</h2>\n<p>Selon les époques, ils peuvent également apprendre le dunael, le haradaïque et les langues successives des peuples cavaliers du Nord : eothrik, eothirric puis rohirrique. Cette diversité linguistique reflète la longue histoire diplomatique, militaire et commerciale du Gondor.</p>\n<h2>Famille</h2>\n<p>Leur formation couvre souvent l’histoire du Gondor et de Númenor, l’organisation militaire, la tactique, le commandement, la poésie, la musique, le chant, les runes, l’astronomie, l’artisanat du métal et de la pierre, l’équitation, la navigation et l’usage de nombreuses armes.</p>\n<h2>Place dans la création de personnage</h2>\n<p>Les Dúnedain du Gondor apprécient les vêtements riches, bien taillés et ornés de cols élaborés. Les habitants des régions chaudes et basses du royaume portent des tuniques légères et peuvent se passer de jambières, tandis que ceux des provinces plus froides adoptent des tenues plus couvrantes.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 17</h2>\n<p>Les hommes portent généralement les cheveux jusqu’aux épaules, les femmes jusqu’à la taille. Les plumes, les motifs blancs sur fond sombre et les symboles héraldiques sont particulièrement appréciés. Les vêtements de cour peuvent associer soie, coton, broderies complexes, ceintures de cuir fin, maillons d’or ou d’argent et capes matelassées à larges manches.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 18</h2>\n<p>L’apparence n’est pas seulement une question de richesse. Elle exprime la maison, le rang, la fonction et l’honneur personnel. Un Dúnadan de haut lignage porte souvent les couleurs et les emblèmes de ses ancêtres, et considère l’entretien de son équipement comme une marque de respect envers son nom.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 19</h2>\n<p>Les guerriers dúnedain ont accès à certaines des meilleures armures fabriquées en Terre du Milieu. Leur grande taille et leur constitution leur permettent de porter des protections lourdes avec plus d’aisance que la plupart des Hommes. Ils emploient un vaste éventail d’armes : épées larges ou longues, épées bâtardes et armes à deux mains, lances, lances de cavalerie, hallebardes, haches de bataille, masses, fléaux, marteaux de guerre, arcs longs ou composites, armes d’hast et mattocks de guerre.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 20</h2>\n<p>Cette diversité reflète une tradition militaire structurée, capable de former aussi bien des fantassins lourds que des archers, des cavaliers, des officiers et des gardes de citadelle.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 21</h2>\n<p>Le mariage est monogame, et la filiation suit traditionnellement la lignée masculine. Les Dúnedain du Gondor peuvent épouser des membres d’autres peuples, ce qui explique l’existence de lignées plus ou moins mêlées.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 22</h2>\n<p>Ces unions ne sont pas nécessairement mal considérées, mais elles peuvent avoir des conséquences sociales importantes dans les milieux les plus attachés à la pureté du sang númenóréen. Les branches mêlées peuvent vivre moins longtemps, développer une barbe et être regardées avec une certaine condescendance par les familles les plus anciennes.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 23</h2>\n<p>Cette Culture convient particulièrement aux personnages issus des maisons royales ou princières, des familles des Intendants, de la noblesse de Minas Tirith, des lignées de Dol Amroth, des grandes maisons militaires ou administratives et des familles dúnedain conservant un lien fort avec Númenor.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 24</h2>\n<p>Elle favorise naturellement les profils de combattants, capitaines, rôdeurs, administrateurs, diplomates, érudits, bardes, alchimistes ou officiers. Les Dúnedain du Gondor constituent ainsi l’expression la plus élevée de l’héritage númenóréen dans le Royaume du Sud : un peuple noble, savant et puissant, mais constamment confronté au poids de son passé, à la peur du déclin et au risque de transformer la conscience de sa grandeur en orgueil.</p><section class=\"merp-ui-starting-languages\" data-merp-ui-starting-languages=\"1\"><h2>Langues de départ</h2><ul><li><strong>Adúnaic</strong> — Parlée 8 / Écrite 6</li><li><strong>Westron</strong> — Parlée 8 / Écrite 6</li><li><strong>Sindarin</strong> — Parlée 6 / Écrite 6</li><li><strong>Quenya</strong> — Parlée 2 / Écrite 2</li></ul></section>","culture":"Dúnedain du Gondor"}},"en":{"name":"Dúnedain (of Gondor)","system":{"description":"<h1>Dúnedain of Gondor</h1>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor are the heirs of the Edain who once received the island of Númenor as a reward for their struggle against Morgoth. Throughout the Second Age, their ancestors developed a powerful maritime civilization, founded ports and colonies along the coasts of Middle-earth, and then extended their influence over much of the West. When Númenor was drowned in the year 3319 of the Second Age, the Faithful who escaped the catastrophe landed notably at Pelargir and took part in the founding of Gondor, the Southern Realm in Exile. The Dúnedain of Gondor thus preserve the heritage of Númenor, mingled with older traces of Elven blood and, according to their own traditions, a distant ancestry connected with the Maiar.</p>\n<h2>Origins</h2>\n<p>This Culture chiefly represents members of the royal, princely, and lordly lineages of Gondor, as well as families that have remained particularly close to the old Númenórean blood. It is especially suitable for heirs of the Stewards, the princes of Dol Amroth, and great houses whose nobility, longevity, and education still distinguish them from ordinary Gondorians. Individuals from more distant branches or more thoroughly mingled with local populations can be represented by weakened Dúnadan ancestry.</p>\n<h2>Lineages and availability</h2>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor are generally very tall and powerfully built. Men often reach nearly six feet four inches, while women frequently approach five feet eleven inches. Their skin is fair, their hair black or very dark brown, and their eyes may be grey, blue, green, or hazel. Men of the purest lineages traditionally grow little or no beard. Their appearance, bearing, and regular features often give them a nobility that is immediately perceptible.</p>\n<h2>Appearance</h2>\n<p>Their endurance is considerable, and their life expectancy far exceeds that of other Men. Depending on the purity of the lineage, a Dúnadan of Gondor may live between one hundred sixty and two hundred forty years. More mixed branches have shorter lifespans, often between ninety and one hundred fifty years, and may display traits closer to those of the kingdom's common populations.</p>\n<h2>Longevity</h2>\n<p>Dúnadan society in Gondor is organized, comfortable, and largely centered on cities, ports, citadels, and fortified estates. Its members are accustomed to living within complex institutions: princely courts, royal administration, a standing army, military orders, guilds, schools of scribes, and noble houses. They grow up in a culture where history, genealogy, law, and service to the realm hold an essential place.</p>\n<h2>Society</h2>\n<p>Their education is demanding. Children of great families learn languages, the history of Númenor, the history of Gondor, politics, the art of war, etiquette, and the conduct of public affairs from an early age. This training explains their confidence, ability to command, and tendency to regard themselves as guardians of a superior tradition.</p>\n<h2>Education</h2>\n<p>This assurance can nevertheless become pride. Many see themselves as the natural guides of the other Men of Middle-earth, whom they sometimes judge less educated, less disciplined, or less civilized. Their nobility of character can then be accompanied by condescension, impatience, or a certain social rigidity.</p>\n<h2>Pride and duty</h2>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor fear death in particular. This fear is not simply that of every mortal faced with the end of life. It also arises from their acute awareness of their own abilities, their longevity, and the great number of works they might yet accomplish.</p>\n<h2>Fear of death</h2>\n<p>The Downfall of Númenor remains at the heart of their collective memory. It constantly reminds them that even the greatest human power can be destroyed by pride and by rejection of the Gift of Ilúvatar. This tension between accepting human destiny and desiring to preserve their work explains part of their gravity, their taste for enduring monuments, their elaborate tombs, and their attachment to lineages.</p>\n<h2>Religion</h2>\n<p>Their fear of death can take different forms: a desire to leave a legacy, obsession with dynastic continuity, refusal of decline, pursuit of glory, or difficulty relinquishing power. Among the wisest, it becomes a sense of duty. Among the proudest, it can feed jealousy, melancholy, or the desire to control the future.</p>\n<h2>Funerary customs</h2>\n<p>The religion of the Dúnedain of Gondor is restrained, personal, and rarely organized around a visible clergy. They revere Eru and all the Valar, with particular devotion to Manwë, Araw — the name given to Oromë — and Varda.</p>\n<h2>Languages</h2>\n<p>Their spirituality is expressed through respect for traditions, oaths, high places, the stars, and memories of Númenor. They distrust excessive religious display and often regard faith as a matter of dignity, memory, and conduct rather than constant public ceremony.</p>\n<h2>Training</h2>\n<p>Their funerals are elaborate and give great importance to preservation of the body. Tombs, mausoleums, and funerary monuments are designed to maintain the memory of the deceased and recall the continuity of the house. This practice reflects both their respect for the dead and their difficulty in fully accepting their disappearance.</p>\n<h2>Clothing</h2>\n<p>Adûnaic remains their ancestral language, but Westron is widely used in daily life and administration. Many also speak Sindarin, while the most educated possess some knowledge of Quenya, especially in historical, ceremonial, and scholarly fields.</p>\n<h2>War</h2>\n<p>Depending on the period, they may also learn Dunael, Haradaic, and the successive languages of the northern horse peoples: Eothrik, Eothirric, and then Rohirric. This linguistic diversity reflects Gondor's long diplomatic, military, and commercial history.</p>\n<h2>Family</h2>\n<p>Their training often covers the history of Gondor and Númenor, military organization, tactics, leadership, poetry, music, song, runes, astronomy, metalcraft and stonecraft, riding, navigation, and the use of numerous weapons.</p>\n<h2>Place in character creation</h2>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor appreciate rich, well-tailored clothing decorated with elaborate collars. In the warmer, lower regions of the kingdom, people wear light tunics and may go without leggings, while those of colder provinces adopt more covering garments.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 17</h2>\n<p>Men generally wear their hair to the shoulders, women to the waist. Feathers, white motifs on dark backgrounds, and heraldic symbols are particularly favored. Court clothing may combine silk, cotton, intricate embroidery, fine leather belts, gold or silver links, and quilted cloaks with broad sleeves.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 18</h2>\n<p>Appearance is not merely a matter of wealth. It expresses house, rank, function, and personal honor. A Dúnadan of high lineage often wears the colors and emblems of his ancestors and regards maintenance of his equipment as a mark of respect toward his name.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 19</h2>\n<p>Dúnedain warriors have access to some of the finest armor made in Middle-earth. Their great stature and constitution allow them to wear heavy protection more easily than most Men. They employ a broad range of weapons: broadswords or longswords, bastard swords and two-handed weapons, spears, cavalry lances, halberds, battle axes, maces, flails, warhammers, long or composite bows, polearms, and war mattocks.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 20</h2>\n<p>This diversity reflects a structured military tradition capable of training heavy infantry, archers, cavalry, officers, and citadel guards alike.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 21</h2>\n<p>Marriage is monogamous, and descent traditionally follows the male line. Dúnedain of Gondor may marry members of other peoples, explaining the existence of lineages of varying degrees of mixed ancestry.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 22</h2>\n<p>Such unions are not necessarily viewed with disfavor, but they can have important social consequences in circles most attached to purity of Númenórean blood. Mixed branches may live shorter lives, grow beards, and be regarded with a degree of condescension by the oldest families.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 23</h2>\n<p>This Culture is particularly suitable for characters from royal or princely houses, families of the Stewards, the nobility of Minas Tirith, the lineages of Dol Amroth, great military or administrative houses, and Dúnedain families retaining a strong connection to Númenor.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 24</h2>\n<p>It naturally favors profiles such as fighters, captains, rangers, administrators, diplomats, scholars, bards, alchemists, or officers. The Dúnedain of Gondor thus represent the highest expression of Númenórean heritage in the Southern Kingdom: a noble, learned, and powerful people, yet constantly confronted by the weight of their past, fear of decline, and the risk of turning awareness of their greatness into pride.</p><section class=\"merp-ui-starting-languages\" data-merp-ui-starting-languages=\"1\"><h2>Starting Languages</h2><ul><li><strong>Adúnaic</strong> — Spoken 8 / Written 6</li><li><strong>Westron</strong> — Spoken 8 / Written 6</li><li><strong>Sindarin</strong> — Spoken 6 / Written 6</li><li><strong>Quenya</strong> — Spoken 2 / Written 2</li></ul></section>","culture":"Dúnedain (of Gondor)"}}}}},"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Dúnedain"],"en":["Dúnedain"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["cultures-dunedain"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU","table":"Conversion RMSS/RMFRP → RMU","culture":"Dúnedain (of Gondor)","description":"<h1>Dúnedain of Gondor</h1>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor are the heirs of the Edain who once received the island of Númenor as a reward for their struggle against Morgoth. Throughout the Second Age, their ancestors developed a powerful maritime civilization, founded ports and colonies along the coasts of Middle-earth, and then extended their influence over much of the West. When Númenor was drowned in the year 3319 of the Second Age, the Faithful who escaped the catastrophe landed notably at Pelargir and took part in the founding of Gondor, the Southern Realm in Exile. The Dúnedain of Gondor thus preserve the heritage of Númenor, mingled with older traces of Elven blood and, according to their own traditions, a distant ancestry connected with the Maiar.</p>\n<h2>Origins</h2>\n<p>This Culture chiefly represents members of the royal, princely, and lordly lineages of Gondor, as well as families that have remained particularly close to the old Númenórean blood. It is especially suitable for heirs of the Stewards, the princes of Dol Amroth, and great houses whose nobility, longevity, and education still distinguish them from ordinary Gondorians. Individuals from more distant branches or more thoroughly mingled with local populations can be represented by weakened Dúnadan ancestry.</p>\n<h2>Lineages and availability</h2>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor are generally very tall and powerfully built. Men often reach nearly six feet four inches, while women frequently approach five feet eleven inches. Their skin is fair, their hair black or very dark brown, and their eyes may be grey, blue, green, or hazel. Men of the purest lineages traditionally grow little or no beard. Their appearance, bearing, and regular features often give them a nobility that is immediately perceptible.</p>\n<h2>Appearance</h2>\n<p>Their endurance is considerable, and their life expectancy far exceeds that of other Men. Depending on the purity of the lineage, a Dúnadan of Gondor may live between one hundred sixty and two hundred forty years. More mixed branches have shorter lifespans, often between ninety and one hundred fifty years, and may display traits closer to those of the kingdom's common populations.</p>\n<h2>Longevity</h2>\n<p>Dúnadan society in Gondor is organized, comfortable, and largely centered on cities, ports, citadels, and fortified estates. Its members are accustomed to living within complex institutions: princely courts, royal administration, a standing army, military orders, guilds, schools of scribes, and noble houses. They grow up in a culture where history, genealogy, law, and service to the realm hold an essential place.</p>\n<h2>Society</h2>\n<p>Their education is demanding. Children of great families learn languages, the history of Númenor, the history of Gondor, politics, the art of war, etiquette, and the conduct of public affairs from an early age. This training explains their confidence, ability to command, and tendency to regard themselves as guardians of a superior tradition.</p>\n<h2>Education</h2>\n<p>This assurance can nevertheless become pride. Many see themselves as the natural guides of the other Men of Middle-earth, whom they sometimes judge less educated, less disciplined, or less civilized. Their nobility of character can then be accompanied by condescension, impatience, or a certain social rigidity.</p>\n<h2>Pride and duty</h2>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor fear death in particular. This fear is not simply that of every mortal faced with the end of life. It also arises from their acute awareness of their own abilities, their longevity, and the great number of works they might yet accomplish.</p>\n<h2>Fear of death</h2>\n<p>The Downfall of Númenor remains at the heart of their collective memory. It constantly reminds them that even the greatest human power can be destroyed by pride and by rejection of the Gift of Ilúvatar. This tension between accepting human destiny and desiring to preserve their work explains part of their gravity, their taste for enduring monuments, their elaborate tombs, and their attachment to lineages.</p>\n<h2>Religion</h2>\n<p>Their fear of death can take different forms: a desire to leave a legacy, obsession with dynastic continuity, refusal of decline, pursuit of glory, or difficulty relinquishing power. Among the wisest, it becomes a sense of duty. Among the proudest, it can feed jealousy, melancholy, or the desire to control the future.</p>\n<h2>Funerary customs</h2>\n<p>The religion of the Dúnedain of Gondor is restrained, personal, and rarely organized around a visible clergy. They revere Eru and all the Valar, with particular devotion to Manwë, Araw — the name given to Oromë — and Varda.</p>\n<h2>Languages</h2>\n<p>Their spirituality is expressed through respect for traditions, oaths, high places, the stars, and memories of Númenor. They distrust excessive religious display and often regard faith as a matter of dignity, memory, and conduct rather than constant public ceremony.</p>\n<h2>Training</h2>\n<p>Their funerals are elaborate and give great importance to preservation of the body. Tombs, mausoleums, and funerary monuments are designed to maintain the memory of the deceased and recall the continuity of the house. This practice reflects both their respect for the dead and their difficulty in fully accepting their disappearance.</p>\n<h2>Clothing</h2>\n<p>Adûnaic remains their ancestral language, but Westron is widely used in daily life and administration. Many also speak Sindarin, while the most educated possess some knowledge of Quenya, especially in historical, ceremonial, and scholarly fields.</p>\n<h2>War</h2>\n<p>Depending on the period, they may also learn Dunael, Haradaic, and the successive languages of the northern horse peoples: Eothrik, Eothirric, and then Rohirric. This linguistic diversity reflects Gondor's long diplomatic, military, and commercial history.</p>\n<h2>Family</h2>\n<p>Their training often covers the history of Gondor and Númenor, military organization, tactics, leadership, poetry, music, song, runes, astronomy, metalcraft and stonecraft, riding, navigation, and the use of numerous weapons.</p>\n<h2>Place in character creation</h2>\n<p>The Dúnedain of Gondor appreciate rich, well-tailored clothing decorated with elaborate collars. In the warmer, lower regions of the kingdom, people wear light tunics and may go without leggings, while those of colder provinces adopt more covering garments.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 17</h2>\n<p>Men generally wear their hair to the shoulders, women to the waist. Feathers, white motifs on dark backgrounds, and heraldic symbols are particularly favored. Court clothing may combine silk, cotton, intricate embroidery, fine leather belts, gold or silver links, and quilted cloaks with broad sleeves.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 18</h2>\n<p>Appearance is not merely a matter of wealth. It expresses house, rank, function, and personal honor. A Dúnadan of high lineage often wears the colors and emblems of his ancestors and regards maintenance of his equipment as a mark of respect toward his name.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 19</h2>\n<p>Dúnedain warriors have access to some of the finest armor made in Middle-earth. Their great stature and constitution allow them to wear heavy protection more easily than most Men. They employ a broad range of weapons: broadswords or longswords, bastard swords and two-handed weapons, spears, cavalry lances, halberds, battle axes, maces, flails, warhammers, long or composite bows, polearms, and war mattocks.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 20</h2>\n<p>This diversity reflects a structured military tradition capable of training heavy infantry, archers, cavalry, officers, and citadel guards alike.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 21</h2>\n<p>Marriage is monogamous, and descent traditionally follows the male line. Dúnedain of Gondor may marry members of other peoples, explaining the existence of lineages of varying degrees of mixed ancestry.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 22</h2>\n<p>Such unions are not necessarily viewed with disfavor, but they can have important social consequences in circles most attached to purity of Númenórean blood. Mixed branches may live shorter lives, grow beards, and be regarded with a degree of condescension by the oldest families.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 23</h2>\n<p>This Culture is particularly suitable for characters from royal or princely houses, families of the Stewards, the nobility of Minas Tirith, the lineages of Dol Amroth, great military or administrative houses, and Dúnedain families retaining a strong connection to Númenor.</p>\n<h2>Additional notes 24</h2>\n<p>It naturally favors profiles such as fighters, captains, rangers, administrators, diplomats, scholars, bards, alchemists, or officers. The Dúnedain of Gondor thus represent the highest expression of Númenórean heritage in the Southern Kingdom: a noble, learned, and powerful people, yet constantly confronted by the weight of their past, fear of decline, and the risk of turning awareness of their greatness into pride.</p><section class=\"merp-ui-starting-languages\" data-merp-ui-starting-languages=\"1\"><h2>Starting Languages</h2><ul><li><strong>Adúnaic</strong> — Spoken 8 / Written 6</li><li><strong>Westron</strong> — Spoken 8 / Written 6</li><li><strong>Sindarin</strong> — Spoken 6 / Written 6</li><li><strong>Quenya</strong> — Spoken 2 / Written 2</li></ul></section>","cultureRanks":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Animal Handling","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Riding","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Perception","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Tracking","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Maneuvering in Armor","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Body Development","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Weight Training","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Unarmed","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Melee Weapons","ranks":4,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Ranged Weapons","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Composition & Performance Art","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Crafting & Vocation","ranks":6,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Navigation","ranks":3},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Piloting","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival","specialization":"Own Region","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Acrobatics","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Jumping","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Languages","ranks":14,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Own","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Neighboring","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Religion/Philosophy","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Other Lores","ranks":5,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Herbalism","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Medicine","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Climbing","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Swimming","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Influence","ranks":4,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Social Awareness","ranks":3},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Trading","ranks":3},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Concealment","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking","ranks":1}]},"_id":"uRLV7PuebdpHYQIP"}
{"name":"Dúnedain (of the Lost Realm)","type":"culture","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"culture-human-dunedain-lost-realm","canonicalKey":"culture-human-dunedain-lost-realm","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":4,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-conversion","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5"},"availability":{"ages":[2,3,4],"restrictedByAge":{"2":false,"3":false,"4":false}},"recommendedSpecializations":{"Environmental — Survival":["Eriador","Forest","Hills"],"Lore — Other Lores":["History of Arnor","Heraldry","Enemy Lore"],"Crafting & Vocation":["Ranger","Scout","Healer","Warden"]},"characterCreation":{"professionRules":{"recommendedProfessionKeys":["ranger","fighter","rogue","layman","bard"]}},"raceCanonicalKey":"race-dunedain","descriptionFormat":"html","compendiumCanonical":true,"ageVariants":{"2":{"sourceKey":"culture-human-dunedain-lost-realm-age-2","folderKey":"cultures-age-2-dunedain","document":{"name":"Dúnedain du Royaume Perdu","type":"culture","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"culture-human-dunedain-lost-realm-age-2","canonicalKey":"culture-human-dunedain-lost-realm","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":4,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-conversion","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5"},"raceKey":"race-dunedain-age-2","availability":{"age":2,"ageLabel":"Deuxième Âge","restricted":false,"startQualifier":"Après 3319 du Deuxième Âge"},"languages":{"starting":[{"name":"Adúnaic","spoken":8,"written":6,"nameFr":"Adûnaïque"},{"name":"Westron","spoken":8,"written":6,"nameFr":"Westron"},{"name":"Sindarin","spoken":6,"written":6,"nameFr":"Sindarin"},{"name":"Quenya","spoken":2,"written":2,"nameFr":"Quenya"}],"source":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5","sourceStatus":"verified"},"recommendedSpecializations":{"Environmental — Survival":["Eriador","Forest","Hills"],"Lore — Other Lores":["History of Arnor","Heraldry","Enemy Lore"],"Crafting & Vocation":["Ranger","Scout","Healer","Warden"]},"characterCreation":{"professionRules":{"recommendedProfessionKeys":["ranger","fighter","rogue","layman","bard"]}},"raceCanonicalKey":"race-dunedain","descriptionFormat":"html"}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU","table":"Conversion RMSS/RMFRP → RMU","culture":"Dúnedain du Royaume Perdu","description":"<h1>Dúnedain du Royaume Perdu</h1>\n<p>Les Dúnedain du Royaume d’Arnor sont les héritiers des Fidèles de Númenor qui suivirent Elendil vers le nord après la Submersion. Là où leurs parents fondèrent le Gondor au sud, eux établirent Arnor, le Royaume du Nord en exil, sur les terres de l’Eriador. Leur histoire est celle d’une grandeur ancienne progressivement brisée par les divisions dynastiques, les guerres contre l’Angmar, l’effondrement des cités et la disparition de presque toutes les institutions qui avaient autrefois fait la puissance des royaumes númenóréens.</p>\n<h2>Origines</h2>\n<p>Cette Culture représente les membres des lignées dúnedain les plus pures d’Arnor et de ses royaumes successeurs — Arthedain, Cardolan et Rhudaur — ainsi que leurs descendants après la chute du Royaume du Nord. Elle convient aussi bien aux nobles et officiers des anciennes cours arnoriannes qu’aux survivants devenus rôdeurs, gardiens de domaines isolés, capitaines de petites communautés ou protecteurs secrets des routes de l’Eriador.</p>\n<h2>Le Royaume du Nord</h2>\n<p>Leur culture est traditionnelle, instruite et issue d’une société fortement organisée. Leur enfance est consacrée à l’apprentissage des langues, de l’histoire, des armes, du commandement et des usages de leur rang. Ils ont accès à toutes les armures et à une grande variété d’armes, tandis que leur équipement, leurs vêtements et leurs objets personnels témoignent encore d’un artisanat raffiné et d’une culture noble.</p>\n<h2>Apparence</h2>\n<p>À son apogée, Arnor couvrait une grande partie de l’Eriador. Ses routes reliaient Annúminas, Fornost, Amon Sûl et les terres occidentales ; ses forteresses surveillaient les frontières ; ses rois descendaient directement d’Elendil et d’Isildur. Pourtant, le royaume se divisa en trois entités rivales, et cette fragmentation affaiblit durablement les Dúnedain du Nord.</p>\n<h2>Diversité des lignées</h2>\n<p>Les descendants d’Arthedain préservèrent le plus longtemps la lignée royale et les traditions d’Elendil. Ceux du Cardolan furent davantage exposés aux guerres, aux migrations et au mélange avec les populations locales. Au Rhudaur, nombre de lignées dúnedain furent marginalisées, corrompues ou détruites lorsque le royaume passa sous l’influence de l’Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Société arnorianne</h2>\n<p>Après la chute de Fornost, les survivants ne disposèrent plus d’un royaume véritable. Les plus fidèles se retirèrent dans les régions sauvages et poursuivirent leur mission sans couronne visible, sans armée permanente et presque sans reconnaissance publique. Cette rupture transforma profondément leur culture : l’apparat de la noblesse arnorianne céda progressivement la place à la discrétion, à la vigilance et au service silencieux.</p>\n<h2>Après la chute</h2>\n<p>Les Dúnedain du Royaume d’Arnor sont grands, robustes et d’allure noble. Leur apparence reste proche de celle des Dúnedain du Gondor : peau claire, cheveux généralement noirs ou brun sombre, yeux gris, bleus, verts ou noisette. Les lignées les plus pures sont souvent imberbes ou peu barbues.</p>\n<h2>Mémoire et devoir</h2>\n<p>Leur espérance de vie maximale est généralement comprise entre cent soixante et deux cents ans, légèrement inférieure à celle des plus hautes lignées du Gondor. Les familles qui se sont mêlées aux populations de l’Eriador peuvent vivre moins longtemps, souvent entre quatre-vingt-dix et cent vingt-cinq ans, avec une résistance moindre aux maladies et une constitution plus proche de celle des Hommes communs.</p>\n<h2>Mode de vie</h2>\n<p>Cette diversité est particulièrement importante dans les anciens territoires du Cardolan et du Rhudaur. Deux personnages se disant Dúnedain peuvent donc partager la même mémoire culturelle sans posséder exactement la même pureté de sang ni la même longévité.</p>\n<h2>Religion</h2>\n<p>À l’époque du royaume, la société arnorianne était raffinée et hiérarchisée. Les familles nobles conservaient des généalogies détaillées, entretenaient des domaines fortifiés et formaient leurs enfants au droit, à l’histoire, à la diplomatie et aux armes. Les Dúnedain accordaient une grande importance aux serments, à la légitimité et à la continuité de la lignée.</p>\n<h2>Langues</h2>\n<p>Après les catastrophes qui frappèrent le Nord, cette noblesse devint plus austère. Les survivants apprirent à vivre avec peu, à protéger des communautés qui ignoraient parfois jusqu’à leur identité et à préserver des traditions dont ils étaient presque les derniers dépositaires. Leur fierté ne disparut pas, mais elle prit une forme plus retenue que chez certains nobles du Gondor.</p>\n<h2>Vêtements</h2>\n<p>Ils se montrent rarement expansifs. Leur réserve vient à la fois de leur éducation, du poids de leurs responsabilités et de la nécessité historique de dissimuler leurs mouvements. Ils parlent peu de leur lignée à ceux qui n’ont pas gagné leur confiance, et préfèrent souvent être pris pour de simples voyageurs, chasseurs ou soldats.</p>\n<h2>Guerre</h2>\n<p>La mémoire de la ruine d’Arnor marque profondément leur tempérament. Là où les Dúnedain du Gondor vivent encore au milieu de cités, de ports et de monuments visibles, ceux du Nord habitent souvent parmi les vestiges de leur propre passé : routes abandonnées, tours effondrées, tertres funéraires, domaines déserts et forteresses occupées par leurs ennemis.</p>\n<h2>Formation</h2>\n<p>Cette proximité avec la perte nourrit chez eux une forme de mélancolie, mais aussi une détermination exceptionnelle. Ils savent que la victoire n’est pas toujours immédiate et qu’une mission peut exiger plusieurs générations. Leur conception du devoir est donc moins éclatante que celle d’un chevalier de cour : elle repose sur l’endurance, la patience et l’acceptation du sacrifice.</p>\n<h2>Place dans la création de personnage</h2>\n<p>Leur ennemi principal demeure l’Ombre venue du Nord : l’Angmar, les Orcs, les Hommes corrompus et les créatures qui hantent les anciennes terres d’Arnor. Même après la disparition du Roi-Sorcier, ils restent attentifs aux signes de son héritage.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 15</h2>\n<p>Avant la chute du royaume, les Dúnedain d’Arnor vivaient dans des villes, des domaines seigneuriaux et des places fortes. Après l’effondrement du Royaume du Nord, beaucoup adoptèrent un mode de vie semi-nomade. Les familles se dispersèrent dans des refuges discrets, des villages protégés ou des domaines isolés. Certains continuèrent d’exploiter des terres ; d’autres devinrent gardiens, éclaireurs, messagers ou chasseurs.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 16</h2>\n<p>Leur autonomie est très développée. Un Dúnadan du Nord apprend souvent à voyager sans attirer l’attention, survivre dans les collines, les forêts et les landes, lire les traces, connaître les anciennes routes, entretenir ses armes, soigner les blessures courantes et agir seul ou au sein d’un petit groupe.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 17</h2>\n<p>Cette adaptation ne signifie pas qu’ils ont abandonné leur culture savante. Beaucoup conservent une connaissance approfondie de l’histoire, des langues et des anciennes institutions d’Arnor.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 18</h2>\n<p>Leur religion reste sobre et personnelle. Ils révèrent Eru et les Valar sans entretenir un clergé puissant ou une religion démonstrative. La fidélité, la vérité, le respect des morts et l’accomplissement du devoir constituent pour eux des expressions essentielles de la foi.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 19</h2>\n<p>Les tombes, les tertres et les monuments des anciens rois sont traités avec un profond respect. Les Dúnedain connaissent souvent les généalogies et les récits liés aux lieux qu’ils traversent. Une ruine que les habitants ordinaires considèrent comme un amas de pierres peut être, pour eux, le dernier vestige d’une maison alliée ou le tombeau d’un capitaine oublié.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 20</h2>\n<p>Comme les Dúnedain du Gondor, ils craignent la mort, mais leur longue expérience de la guerre et du déclin les rend peut-être plus résignés à son égard. Ils cherchent moins à la vaincre qu’à faire en sorte que leur œuvre leur survive.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 21</h2>\n<p>Le westron est leur langue quotidienne, tandis que l’adûnaïque demeure une langue ancestrale et savante. Le sindarin tient une place importante dans les familles nobles, les échanges avec Fondcombe et les traditions historiques. Le quenya est surtout réservé aux textes anciens, aux cérémonies et aux érudits.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 22</h2>\n<p>Selon leur région et leur époque, ils peuvent également connaître le dunael, le blarm, les parlers eriadoriens, les langues des Hommes du Nord et certaines formes de communication utilisées par les éclaireurs et les gardiens.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 23</h2>\n<p>Leurs vêtements traditionnels sont riches et élaborés : sous-vêtements de lin, de soie ou de laine, tuniques aux couleurs profondes, manches larges, broderies, ceintures ornées, capes matelassées et manteaux de fourrure. Après la chute d’Arnor, les Dúnedain adaptent cet héritage à une vie plus discrète : couleurs sombres ou naturelles, manteaux de voyage, bottes robustes, vêtements renforcés, bijoux et broderies conservés sous les habits ordinaires, emblèmes familiaux rarement montrés en public.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 24</h2>\n<p>Les objets les plus précieux sont souvent transmis de génération en génération : épées, broches, anneaux, fourreaux, fragments de bannières ou sceaux de maisons disparues.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 25</h2>\n<p>Les Dúnedain d’Arnor ont accès à toutes les armures et possèdent la constitution nécessaire pour porter les plus lourdes. Leur arsenal traditionnel comprend des épées larges, longues ou bâtardes, des haches de bataille, des lances, des arcs longs et composites, des hallebardes, des masses, des fléaux, des marteaux de guerre, des armes à deux mains et des mattocks de guerre.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 26</h2>\n<p>Après la chute du royaume, ils privilégient cependant souvent un équipement plus mobile : épée, arc, lance courte, bouclier léger, cotte de mailles et manteau de voyage. Ils excellent dans les patrouilles, les embuscades, la reconnaissance, la défense de passages, la chasse aux Orcs, la protection de voyageurs et les opérations menées par de petits groupes.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 27</h2>\n<p>Leur éducation couvre la diplomatie, l’histoire, le commandement, la médecine, la musique, les langues, les runes, l’équitation, la tactique, le pistage, la navigation et de nombreux arts militaires ou artisanaux.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 28</h2>\n<p>Cette Culture convient particulièrement aux personnages issus des anciennes familles d’Arthedain, des survivants du Cardolan, des lignées dúnedain de l’Angle, des descendants des nobles du Rhudaur demeurés fidèles, des Rôdeurs du Nord, des gardiens de refuges et de domaines cachés ou des familles protégeant les héritiers d’Isildur.</p>\n<h2>Compléments 29</h2>\n<p>Les Dúnedain d’Arnor sont ainsi les gardiens d’un royaume qui n’existe plus. Leur identité repose moins sur les terres qu’ils gouvernent encore que sur les serments qu’ils refusent d’abandonner. Ils vivent entre les ruines de leur passé et l’espoir lointain d’une restauration, poursuivant dans l’ombre une mission que la plupart des habitants de l’Eriador ne soupçonnent même pas.</p><section class=\"merp-ui-starting-languages\" data-merp-ui-starting-languages=\"1\"><h2>Langues de départ</h2><ul><li><strong>Adúnaic</strong> — Parlée 8 / Écrite 6</li><li><strong>Westron</strong> — Parlée 8 / Écrite 6</li><li><strong>Sindarin</strong> — Parlée 6 / Écrite 6</li><li><strong>Quenya</strong> — Parlée 2 / Écrite 2</li></ul></section>","cultureRanks":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Animal Handling","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Riding","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Perception","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Tracking","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Maneuvering in Armor","ranks":3},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Body Development","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Weight Training","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Unarmed","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Melee Weapons","ranks":4,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Ranged Weapons","ranks":3,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Composition & Performance Art","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Crafting & Vocation","ranks":4,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Navigation","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Piloting","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival","specialization":"Eriador","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Acrobatics","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Jumping","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Languages","ranks":12,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Own","ranks":6},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Neighboring","ranks":6},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Religion/Philosophy","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Other Lores","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Herbalism","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Medicine","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Climbing","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Swimming","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Influence","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Social Awareness","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Trading","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Concealment","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking","ranks":3}]}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Dúnedain du Royaume Perdu","system":{"description":"<h1>Dúnedain du Royaume Perdu</h1>\n<p>Les Dúnedain du Royaume d’Arnor sont les héritiers des Fidèles de Númenor qui suivirent Elendil vers le nord après la Submersion. Là où leurs parents fondèrent le Gondor au sud, eux établirent Arnor, le Royaume du Nord en exil, sur les terres de l’Eriador. Leur histoire est celle d’une grandeur ancienne progressivement brisée par les divisions dynastiques, les guerres contre l’Angmar, l’effondrement des cités et la disparition de presque toutes les institutions qui avaient autrefois fait la puissance des royaumes númenóréens.</p>\n<h2>Origines</h2>\n<p>Cette Culture représente les membres des lignées dúnedain les plus pures d’Arnor et de ses royaumes successeurs — Arthedain, Cardolan et Rhudaur — ainsi que leurs descendants après la chute du Royaume du Nord. Elle convient aussi bien aux nobles et officiers des anciennes cours arnoriannes qu’aux survivants devenus rôdeurs, gardiens de domaines isolés, capitaines de petites communautés ou protecteurs secrets des routes de l’Eriador.</p>\n<h2>Le Royaume du Nord</h2>\n<p>Leur culture est traditionnelle, instruite et issue d’une société fortement organisée. Leur enfance est consacrée à l’apprentissage des langues, de l’histoire, des armes, du commandement et des usages de leur rang. Ils ont ac