rsque l’occasion se présente.</p>\n<h2>Société et famille</h2>\n<p>La société reste très simple. Autorité, propriété et conflits sont réglés de manière concrète, souvent par la force. Les structures politiques complexes, administrations et institutions savantes sont absentes. Les formes de famille et de groupement varient selon la population troll, mais l’apprentissage reste presque entièrement pratique.</p>\n<h2>Tempérament</h2>\n<p>Les Trolls sont généralement lents, brutaux, peu sophistiqués et quelque peu paresseux. Ils peuvent néanmoins apprendre des habitudes efficaces lorsque celles-ci répondent directement à la nourriture, au territoire, au combat ou à une contrainte imposée par un maître. Leur intelligence limitée n’empêche pas l’expérience pratique.</p>\n<h2>Langues</h2>\n<p>La capacité linguistique est faible chez la plupart des Tereg primitifs. La communication repose beaucoup sur bruits, gestes et actions. Les individus les plus intelligents peuvent apprendre westron dégradé, dunael et éventuellement orcien. La lecture et l’écriture restent marginales et les concepts concrets sont nettement plus faciles à exprimer que les idées abstraites.</p>\n<h2>Vêtements et artisanat</h2>\n<p>Les vêtements sont rares et grossiers, principalement faits de peaux et de cuir. Le Travail du cuir et le Travail de la pierre restent utilitaires : sangle, manteau, arme simple, agrandissement d’un refuge ou déplacement de blocs. La finesse artisanale et la décoration ne sont pas des priorités.</p>\n<h2>Armes et armures</h2>\n<p>Les armes favorisent la masse et la simplicité : gourdins, masses, marteaux, pierres ou projectiles improvisés selon la population. Les griffes et autres armes naturelles appartiennent à la Race mais leur usage peut être développé par la Culture. Les armures lourdes sont rares chez les Trolls primitifs et proviennent généralement d’un maître extérieur lorsqu’elles existent.</p>\n<h2>Magie et religion</h2>\n<p>Les Tereg primitifs ne possèdent normalement ni religion structurée ni tradition magique autonome. Ils peuvent servir une puissance de l’Ombre par peur ou contrainte. Les objets enchantés qu’ils possèdent ont presque toujours été trouvés ou volés et peuvent rester incompris.</p>\n<h2>Relations</h2>\n<p>Les Khazâd constituent des ennemis particulièrement détestés dans de nombreuses traditions troll. Les relations avec Orcs et Humains dépendent du contexte : proies, rivaux, alliés forcés ou maîtres. Une puissance extérieure suffisamment forte peut employer un Troll comme garde, soldat ou force de choc.</p>\n<h2>Compétences culturelles</h2>\n<p>Le profil favorise <strong>Armes de percussion, Armes de jet, Combat naturel, Développement corporel, Pistage, Spéléologie, Dissimulation et Travail de la pierre</strong>. Escalade, Approche furtive, Fabrication de pièges, Cueillette, Travail du cuir, connaissance de la faune et Jeu complètent le profil. Si une compétence d’évaluation pratique du butin est disponible, elle est particulièrement cohérente.</p>\n<h2>Race et Culture</h2>\n<p>La Race Troll porte stature, force, peau naturelle, griffes, sens, résistances et réaction au Soleil. La Culture porte environnement, chasse, armes apprises, langues, comportement social, artisanat rudimentaire et compétences de terrain. Les mêmes avantages physiologiques ne doivent jamais être comptés deux fois.</p>\n<h2>Disponibilité et création</h2>\n<p>Cette Culture est disponible aux <strong>Premier, Deuxième, Troisième et Quatrième Âges</strong>. Un personnage joueur représente généralement un individu exceptionnel, capable de communiquer suffisamment avec un groupe. Sa tanière, son ancien maître éventuel, sa relation aux autres Trolls et une motivation concrète — nourriture, territoire, vengeance, perte d’un refuge ou butin — définissent immédiatement son histoire.</p>","culture":"Trolls de Pierre"}},"en":{"name":"Stone Trolls","system":{"description":"<h1>Stone Trolls</h1>\n<p><strong>Stone Trolls</strong> are one of the most common forms of Torog. Their Culture is particularly marked by the accumulation of food and treasure, theft between Trolls, boasting, and extreme fratricidal tendencies. They live in caves and ruins near civilized regions, hunting, pillaging, and jealously protecting whatever they consider their property.</p>\n<h2>Way of life</h2>\n<p>A Stone Troll wants to possess things. Food, weapons, coins, gems, shiny objects, or simple trinkets taken from a victim can all enter its hoard. Economic value matters less than ownership and the ability to boast. Trolls regularly steal from one another, feeding jealousy, hidden caches, quarrels, and murder.</p>\n<p>They form almost no durable society without outside coercion. Strength settles disputes, and a large group quickly becomes unstable. They live to eat and “play,” which often means killing, looting, gambling, or fighting. Their food includes fresh meat and crude preparations, with a few peculiar habits such as jellied entrails.</p>\n<p>Their equipment is scavenged or made very crudely: hide cloaks, stolen pieces of armor, clubs, maces, hammers, bludgeons, and boulders. Weapons may combine wood, stone, and sinew bindings. Possessions accumulate around the lair until they form genuine hoards.</p>\n<h2>Shelter and sunlight</h2>\n<p>The lair or refuge must provide effective protection from sunlight. This vulnerability belongs to the Race, but it shapes the whole Culture: nocturnal activity, knowledge of shelters, attention to sunrise, and a preference for caves, ruins, ravines, or other covered places. An experienced Troll knows several possible refuges within its territory.</p>\n<h2>Food and hunting</h2>\n<p>Food is a fundamental motivation. Meat predominates, supplemented according to the environment by game, scavenged resources, and the spoils of raids. Hunting develops Tracking, patience, knowledge of the terrain, and sometimes trapping. Members of other peoples can become prey when the opportunity arises.</p>\n<h2>Society and family</h2>\n<p>Society remains very simple. Authority, property, and disputes are settled in concrete ways, often by force. Complex political structures, administrations, and scholarly institutions are absent. Forms of family and grouping vary among Troll populations, but learning remains almost entirely practical.</p>\n<h2>Temperament</h2>\n<p>Trolls are generally slow, brutal, unsophisticated, and somewhat lazy. They can nevertheless learn effective habits when those habits directly concern food, territory, combat, or a constraint imposed by a master. Limited intelligence does not prevent practical experience.</p>\n<h2>Languages</h2>\n<p>Linguistic ability is poor among most primitive Tereg. Communication relies heavily on noises, gestures, and actions. The more intelligent individuals can learn broken Westron, Dunael, and possibly Orcish. Reading and writing remain marginal, and concrete concepts are far easier to express than abstract ideas.</p>\n<h2>Clothing and craft</h2>\n<p>Clothing is rare and crude, made mainly from hides and leather. Leatherworking and Stoneworking remain utilitarian: straps, cloaks, simple weapons, enlarging a refuge, or moving blocks of stone. Fine craftsmanship and decoration are not priorities.</p>\n<h2>Weapons and armor</h2>\n<p>Weapons favor mass and simplicity: clubs, maces, hammers, stones, or improvised missiles depending on the population. Claws and other natural weapons belong to the Race, but their use can be developed through Culture. Heavy armor is rare among primitive Trolls and, when it exists, usually comes from an outside master.</p>\n<h2>Magic and religion</h2>\n<p>Primitive Tereg normally possess neither an organized religion nor an independent magical tradition. They may serve a power of the Shadow through fear or coercion. Enchanted objects in their possession have almost always been found or stolen and may remain poorly understood.</p>\n<h2>Relations</h2>\n<p>Khazâd are particularly hated enemies in many Troll traditions. Relations with Orcs and Humans depend on circumstance: prey, rivals, forced allies, or masters. A sufficiently powerful outside force can employ a Troll as a guard, soldier, or shock troop.</p>\n<h2>Cultural skills</h2>\n<p>The profile favors <strong>Concussion Weapons, Thrown Weapons, Natural Combat, Body Development, Tracking, Caving, Hiding, and Stoneworking</strong>. Climbing, Stalking, Trapmaking, Foraging, Leatherworking, knowledge of wildlife, and Gaming round out the profile. If a skill for practical appraisal of loot is available, it is particularly appropriate.</p>\n<h2>Race and Culture</h2>\n<p>The Troll Race carries stature, strength, natural hide, claws, senses, resistances, and reaction to the Sun. Culture carries environment, hunting, learned weapons, languages, social behavior, rudimentary craft, and terrain skills. The same physiological benefits must never be counted twice.</p>\n<h2>Availability and character creation</h2>\n<p>This Culture is available in the <strong>First, Second, Third, and Fourth Ages</strong>. A player character generally represents an exceptional individual capable of communicating well enough with a group. Its lair, any former master, its relationship with other Trolls, and a concrete motivation — food, territory, revenge, the loss of a refuge, or loot — immediately define its story.</p>","culture":"Stone Trolls"}}}}},"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Restreint"],"en":["Restricted"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["cultures-restricted"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU","table":"Conversion RMSS/RMFRP → RMU","culture":"Stone Trolls","description":"<h1>Stone Trolls</h1>\n<p><strong>Stone Trolls</strong> are one of the most common forms of Torog. Their Culture is particularly marked by the accumulation of food and treasure, theft between Trolls, boasting, and extreme fratricidal tendencies. They live in caves and ruins near civilized regions, hunting, pillaging, and jealously protecting whatever they consider their property.</p>\n<h2>Way of life</h2>\n<p>A Stone Troll wants to possess things. Food, weapons, coins, gems, shiny objects, or simple trinkets taken from a victim can all enter its hoard. Economic value matters less than ownership and the ability to boast. Trolls regularly steal from one another, feeding jealousy, hidden caches, quarrels, and murder.</p>\n<p>They form almost no durable society without outside coercion. Strength settles disputes, and a large group quickly becomes unstable. They live to eat and “play,” which often means killing, looting, gambling, or fighting. Their food includes fresh meat and crude preparations, with a few peculiar habits such as jellied entrails.</p>\n<p>Their equipment is scavenged or made very crudely: hide cloaks, stolen pieces of armor, clubs, maces, hammers, bludgeons, and boulders. Weapons may combine wood, stone, and sinew bindings. Possessions accumulate around the lair until they form genuine hoards.</p>\n<h2>Shelter and sunlight</h2>\n<p>The lair or refuge must provide effective protection from sunlight. This vulnerability belongs to the Race, but it shapes the whole Culture: nocturnal activity, knowledge of shelters, attention to sunrise, and a preference for caves, ruins, ravines, or other covered places. An experienced Troll knows several possible refuges within its territory.</p>\n<h2>Food and hunting</h2>\n<p>Food is a fundamental motivation. Meat predominates, supplemented according to the environment by game, scavenged resources, and the spoils of raids. Hunting develops Tracking, patience, knowledge of the terrain, and sometimes trapping. Members of other peoples can become prey when the opportunity arises.</p>\n<h2>Society and family</h2>\n<p>Society remains very simple. Authority, property, and disputes are settled in concrete ways, often by force. Complex political structures, administrations, and scholarly institutions are absent. Forms of family and grouping vary among Troll populations, but learning remains almost entirely practical.</p>\n<h2>Temperament</h2>\n<p>Trolls are generally slow, brutal, unsophisticated, and somewhat lazy. They can nevertheless learn effective habits when those habits directly concern food, territory, combat, or a constraint imposed by a master. Limited intelligence does not prevent practical experience.</p>\n<h2>Languages</h2>\n<p>Linguistic ability is poor among most primitive Tereg. Communication relies heavily on noises, gestures, and actions. The more intelligent individuals can learn broken Westron, Dunael, and possibly Orcish. Reading and writing remain marginal, and concrete concepts are far easier to express than abstract ideas.</p>\n<h2>Clothing and craft</h2>\n<p>Clothing is rare and crude, made mainly from hides and leather. Leatherworking and Stoneworking remain utilitarian: straps, cloaks, simple weapons, enlarging a refuge, or moving blocks of stone. Fine craftsmanship and decoration are not priorities.</p>\n<h2>Weapons and armor</h2>\n<p>Weapons favor mass and simplicity: clubs, maces, hammers, stones, or improvised missiles depending on the population. Claws and other natural weapons belong to the Race, but their use can be developed through Culture. Heavy armor is rare among primitive Trolls and, when it exists, usually comes from an outside master.</p>\n<h2>Magic and religion</h2>\n<p>Primitive Tereg normally possess neither an organized religion nor an independent magical tradition. They may serve a power of the Shadow through fear or coercion. Enchanted objects in their possession have almost always been found or stolen and may remain poorly understood.</p>\n<h2>Relations</h2>\n<p>Khazâd are particularly hated enemies in many Troll traditions. Relations with Orcs and Humans depend on circumstance: prey, rivals, forced allies, or masters. A sufficiently powerful outside force can employ a Troll as a guard, soldier, or shock troop.</p>\n<h2>Cultural skills</h2>\n<p>The profile favors <strong>Concussion Weapons, Thrown Weapons, Natural Combat, Body Development, Tracking, Caving, Hiding, and Stoneworking</strong>. Climbing, Stalking, Trapmaking, Foraging, Leatherworking, knowledge of wildlife, and Gaming round out the profile. If a skill for practical appraisal of loot is available, it is particularly appropriate.</p>\n<h2>Race and Culture</h2>\n<p>The Troll Race carries stature, strength, natural hide, claws, senses, resistances, and reaction to the Sun. Culture carries environment, hunting, learned weapons, languages, social behavior, rudimentary craft, and terrain skills. The same physiological benefits must never be counted twice.</p>\n<h2>Availability and character creation</h2>\n<p>This Culture is available in the <strong>First, Second, Third, and Fourth Ages</strong>. A player character generally represents an exceptional individual capable of communicating well enough with a group. Its lair, any former master, its relationship with other Trolls, and a concrete motivation — food, territory, revenge, the loss of a refuge, or loot — immediately define its story.</p>","cultureRanks":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Animal Handling","ranks":0,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Riding","ranks":0,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Perception","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Tracking","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Maneuvering in Armor","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Body Development","ranks":6},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Weight Training","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Unarmed","ranks":6,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Melee Weapons","ranks":7,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Ranged Weapons","ranks":5,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Composition & Performance Art","ranks":0,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Crafting & Vocation","ranks":3,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Navigation","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Piloting","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival","specialization":"Hills","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Acrobatics","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Jumping","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Languages","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Own","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Neighboring","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Religion/Philosophy","ranks":0,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Other Lores","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Herbalism","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Medicine","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Climbing","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Swimming","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Influence","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Social Awareness","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Trading","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Concealment","ranks":4},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking","ranks":3}]},"_id":"Tb14kQ6LvKBbNEFp"}
{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","type":"culture","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"culture-teleri","canonicalKey":"culture-teleri","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":1,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-conversion","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5"},"raceCanonicalKey":"race-elves","availability":{"ages":[1],"restrictedByAge":{"1":false}},"descriptionFormat":"html","compendiumCanonical":true,"ageVariants":{"1":{"sourceKey":"culture-teleri-age-1","folderKey":"cultures-age-1-elves","document":{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","type":"culture","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"culture-teleri-age-1","canonicalKey":"culture-teleri","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":1,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-conversion","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5"},"raceKey":"race-elves-age-1","raceCanonicalKey":"race-elves","availability":{"age":1,"ageLabel":"Premier Âge","restricted":false},"descriptionFormat":"html","languages":{"starting":[],"source":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5","sourceStatus":"starting-languages-not-resolved"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU","table":"Conversion RMSS/RMFRP → RMU","culture":"Teleri — Falmari","description":"<h1>Teleri — Falmari</h1>\n<p>Les <strong>Teleri</strong> constituent la troisième et la plus nombreuse des grandes lignées des Eldar ayant entrepris la Grande Marche. Ceux qui atteignirent Aman sont appelés <strong>Falmari</strong>, le « Peuple des Vagues ». Leur Culture est inséparable d’Alqualondë, de la mer, du chant, de la construction navale et des navires-cygnes qui comptent parmi les plus belles œuvres des Eldar.</p>\n<h2>Grande Marche et divisions</h2>\n<p>Les Teleri progressèrent plus lentement que les Vanyar et les Noldor, s’arrêtant volontiers dans les terres rencontrées. Plusieurs branches se séparèrent : les Nandor abandonnèrent la Marche plus tôt, tandis que les Sindar descendirent des Teleri restés en Beleriand. Les Falmari suivirent Olwë jusqu’en Aman.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë</h2>\n<p>Alqualondë, le Havre des Cygnes, est leur grande cité côtière. Son architecture accompagne les quais, les arches et la lumière de la mer. Les navires blancs en forme de cygne sont des œuvres uniques où se mêlent charpenterie, sculpture, gréement et art.</p>\n<h2>Mer et navigation</h2>\n<p>Navigation, construction navale, cordages, Natation, Observation des étoiles et Prévision du temps sont au cœur de leur formation. La mer constitue à la fois milieu de vie, source d’inspiration et objet de respect. Ulmo, Ossë et Uinen occupent naturellement une place importante dans leur mémoire et leurs chants.</p>\n<h2>Musique et artisanat</h2>\n<p>Le chant est l’un de leurs arts majeurs. Poésie, musique, perles, nacre, bois et tissus maritimes définissent une esthétique moins tournée vers les gemmes et la forge que celle des Noldor. Le blanc, l’argent, le bleu et les nuances de la mer dominent volontiers les vêtements et ornements.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë et les Noldor</h2>\n<p>Le Massacre d’Alqualondë marque une rupture profonde. Le refus d’Olwë de céder les navires de son peuple conduit Fëanor et ses partisans à employer la force. La mémoire de ce premier Fratricide pèse durablement sur les relations avec les Noldor exilés.</p>\n<h2>Guerre</h2>\n<p>Les Teleri ne structurent pas leur société autour de la guerre. Lorsqu’ils doivent combattre, ils privilégient mobilité, précision, arcs, lances, javelots et lames maniables adaptées aux quais et aux navires. Les protections légères conviennent mieux à leur environnement.</p>\n<h2>Place dans la création de personnage</h2>\n<p>La Culture Teleri est destinée au Premier Âge. Un personnage doit être rattaché à Alqualondë, Tol Eressëa ou aux rivages d’Aman, avec une date précise qui détermine son rapport aux Noldor et au Massacre d’Alqualondë.</p>","cultureRanks":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Animal Handling","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Riding","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Perception","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Tracking","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Maneuvering in Armor","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Body Development","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Weight Training","ranks":0},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Unarmed","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Melee Weapons","ranks":2,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillGroup":"Ranged Weapons","ranks":3,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Composition & Performance Art","ranks":7,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Crafting & Vocation","ranks":7,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Navigation","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Piloting","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival","specialization":"Coastlands","ranks":3},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Acrobatics","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Jumping","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Languages","ranks":10,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Own","ranks":3},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore","specialization":"Neighboring","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Religion/Philosophy","ranks":3,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillGroup":"Other Lores","ranks":0,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Herbalism","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Medicine","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Climbing","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Swimming","ranks":5},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Influence","ranks":1,"maxTwoRanksPerSpecialization":true},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Social Awareness","ranks":2},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Trading","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Concealment","ranks":1},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking","ranks":2}]}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","system":{"description":"<h1>Teleri — Falmari</h1>\n<p>Les <strong>Teleri</strong> constituent la troisième et la plus nombreuse des grandes lignées des Eldar ayant entrepris la Grande Marche. Ceux qui atteignirent Aman sont appelés <strong>Falmari</strong>, le « Peuple des Vagues ». Leur Culture est inséparable d’Alqualondë, de la mer, du chant, de la construction navale et des navires-cygnes qui comptent parmi les plus belles œuvres des Eldar.</p>\n<h2>Grande Marche et divisions</h2>\n<p>Les Teleri progressèrent plus lentement que les Vanyar et les Noldor, s’arrêtant volontiers dans les terres rencontrées. Plusieurs branches se séparèrent : les Nandor abandonnèrent la Marche plus tôt, tandis que les Sindar descendirent des Teleri restés en Beleriand. Les Falmari suivirent Olwë jusqu’en Aman.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë</h2>\n<p>Alqualondë, le Havre des Cygnes, est leur grande cité côtière. Son architecture accompagne les quais, les arches et la lumière de la mer. Les navires blancs en forme de cygne sont des œuvres uniques où se mêlent charpenterie, sculpture, gréement et art.</p>\n<h2>Mer et navigation</h2>\n<p>Navigation, construction navale, cordages, Natation, Observation des étoiles et Prévision du temps sont au cœur de leur formation. La mer constitue à la fois milieu de vie, source d’inspiration et objet de respect. Ulmo, Ossë et Uinen occupent naturellement une place importante dans leur mémoire et leurs chants.</p>\n<h2>Musique et artisanat</h2>\n<p>Le chant est l’un de leurs arts majeurs. Poésie, musique, perles, nacre, bois et tissus maritimes définissent une esthétique moins tournée vers les gemmes et la forge que celle des Noldor. Le blanc, l’argent, le bleu et les nuances de la mer dominent volontiers les vêtements et ornements.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë et les Noldor</h2>\n<p>Le Massacre d’Alqualondë marque une rupture profonde. Le refus d’Olwë de céder les navires de son peuple conduit Fëanor et ses partisans à employer la force. La mémoire de ce premier Fratricide pèse durablement sur les relations avec les Noldor exilés.</p>\n<h2>Guerre</h2>\n<p>Les Teleri ne structurent pas leur société autour de la guerre. Lorsqu’ils doivent combattre, ils privilégient mobilité, précision, arcs, lances, javelots et lames maniables adaptées aux quais et aux navires. Les protections légères conviennent mieux à leur environnement.</p>\n<h2>Place dans la création de personnage</h2>\n<p>La Culture Teleri est destinée au Premier Âge. Un personnage doit être rattaché à Alqualondë, Tol Eressëa ou aux rivages d’Aman, avec une date précise qui détermine son rapport aux Noldor et au Massacre d’Alqualondë.</p>","culture":"Teleri — Falmari"}},"en":{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","system":{"description":"<h1>Teleri — Falmari</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Teleri</strong> are the third and most numerous of the great hosts of the Eldar who undertook the Great Journey. Those who reached Aman are called <strong>Falmari</strong>, the “People of the Waves”. Their Culture is inseparable from Alqualondë, the sea, song, shipbuilding and the swan-ships that rank among the most beautiful works of the Eldar.</p>\n<h2>The Great Journey and divisions</h2>\n<p>The Teleri advanced more slowly than the Vanyar and Noldor, willingly lingering in the lands they encountered. Several branches separated: the Nandor abandoned the Journey earlier, while the Sindar descended from Teleri who remained in Beleriand. The Falmari followed Olwë into Aman.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë</h2>\n<p>Alqualondë, the Haven of the Swans, is their great coastal city. Its architecture follows quays, arches and the light of the sea. The white swan-shaped ships are unique works combining carpentry, carving, rigging and art.</p>\n<h2>Sea and navigation</h2>\n<p>Navigation, Shipbuilding, Ropework, Swimming, Observation of the Stars and Weather Forecasting lie at the heart of their training. The sea is at once their living environment, source of inspiration and object of reverence. Ulmo, Ossë and Uinen naturally occupy important places in their memory and songs.</p>\n<h2>Music and craftsmanship</h2>\n<p>Song is one of their major arts. Poetry, music, pearls, mother-of-pearl, wood and maritime textiles define an aesthetic less focused on gems and smithcraft than that of the Noldor. White, silver, blue and the colours of the sea readily dominate clothing and ornaments.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë and the Noldor</h2>\n<p>The Kinslaying at Alqualondë marks a profound rupture. Olwë's refusal to surrender his people's ships led Fëanor and his followers to use force. The memory of this first Kinslaying weighs for a long time upon relations with the exiled Noldor.</p>\n<h2>War</h2>\n<p>The Teleri do not organise their society around war. When they must fight, they favour mobility, precision, bows, spears, javelins and manageable blades suited to quays and ships. Light protection better suits their environment.</p>\n<h2>Place in character creation</h2>\n<p>Teleri Culture is intended for the First Age. A character should be tied to Alqualondë, Tol Eressëa or the shores of Aman, with a precise date determining their relationship with the Noldor and the Kinslaying at Alqualondë.</p>","culture":"Teleri — Falmari"}}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"culture-teleri","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-cultures","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":null,"compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","system":{"description":"<h1>Teleri — Falmari</h1>\n<p>Les <strong>Teleri</strong> constituent la troisième et la plus nombreuse des grandes lignées des Eldar ayant entrepris la Grande Marche. Ceux qui atteignirent Aman sont appelés <strong>Falmari</strong>, le « Peuple des Vagues ». Leur Culture est inséparable d’Alqualondë, de la mer, du chant, de la construction navale et des navires-cygnes qui comptent parmi les plus belles œuvres des Eldar.</p>\n<h2>Grande Marche et divisions</h2>\n<p>Les Teleri progressèrent plus lentement que les Vanyar et les Noldor, s’arrêtant volontiers dans les terres rencontrées. Plusieurs branches se séparèrent : les Nandor abandonnèrent la Marche plus tôt, tandis que les Sindar descendirent des Teleri restés en Beleriand. Les Falmari suivirent Olwë jusqu’en Aman.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë</h2>\n<p>Alqualondë, le Havre des Cygnes, est leur grande cité côtière. Son architecture accompagne les quais, les arches et la lumière de la mer. Les navires blancs en forme de cygne sont des œuvres uniques où se mêlent charpenterie, sculpture, gréement et art.</p>\n<h2>Mer et navigation</h2>\n<p>Navigation, construction navale, cordages, Natation, Observation des étoiles et Prévision du temps sont au cœur de leur formation. La mer constitue à la fois milieu de vie, source d’inspiration et objet de respect. Ulmo, Ossë et Uinen occupent naturellement une place importante dans leur mémoire et leurs chants.</p>\n<h2>Musique et artisanat</h2>\n<p>Le chant est l’un de leurs arts majeurs. Poésie, musique, perles, nacre, bois et tissus maritimes définissent une esthétique moins tournée vers les gemmes et la forge que celle des Noldor. Le blanc, l’argent, le bleu et les nuances de la mer dominent volontiers les vêtements et ornements.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë et les Noldor</h2>\n<p>Le Massacre d’Alqualondë marque une rupture profonde. Le refus d’Olwë de céder les navires de son peuple conduit Fëanor et ses partisans à employer la force. La mémoire de ce premier Fratricide pèse durablement sur les relations avec les Noldor exilés.</p>\n<h2>Guerre</h2>\n<p>Les Teleri ne structurent pas leur société autour de la guerre. Lorsqu’ils doivent combattre, ils privilégient mobilité, précision, arcs, lances, javelots et lames maniables adaptées aux quais et aux navires. Les protections légères conviennent mieux à leur environnement.</p>\n<h2>Place dans la création de personnage</h2>\n<p>La Culture Teleri est destinée au Premier Âge. Un personnage doit être rattaché à Alqualondë, Tol Eressëa ou aux rivages d’Aman, avec une date précise qui détermine son rapport aux Noldor et au Massacre d’Alqualondë.</p>","culture":"Teleri — Falmari"}},"en":{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","system":{"description":"<h1>Teleri — Falmari</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Teleri</strong> are the third and most numerous of the great hosts of the Eldar who undertook the Great Journey. Those who reached Aman are called <strong>Falmari</strong>, the “People of the Waves”. Their Culture is inseparable from Alqualondë, the sea, song, shipbuilding and the swan-ships that rank among the most beautiful works of the Eldar.</p>\n<h2>The Great Journey and divisions</h2>\n<p>The Teleri advanced more slowly than the Vanyar and Noldor, willingly lingering in the lands they encountered. Several branches separated: the Nandor abandoned the Journey earlier, while the Sindar descended from Teleri who remained in Beleriand. The Falmari followed Olwë into Aman.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë</h2>\n<p>Alqualondë, the Haven of the Swans, is their great coastal city. Its architecture follows quays, arches and the light of the sea. The white swan-shaped ships are unique works combining carpentry, carving, rigging and art.</p>\n<h2>Sea and navigation</h2>\n<p>Navigation, Shipbuilding, Ropework, Swimming, Observation of the Stars and Weather Forecasting lie at the heart of their training. The sea is at once their living environment, source of inspiration and object of reverence. Ulmo, Ossë and Uinen naturally occupy important places in their memory and songs.</p>\n<h2>Music and craftsmanship</h2>\n<p>Song is one of their major arts. Poetry, music, pearls, mother-of-pearl, wood and maritime textiles define an aesthetic less focused on gems and smithcraft than that of the Noldor. White, silver, blue and the colours of the sea readily dominate clothing and ornaments.</p>\n<h2>Alqualondë and the Noldor</h2>\n<p>The Kinslaying at Alqualondë marks a profound rupture. Olwë's refusal to surrender his people's ships led Fëanor and his followers to use force. The memory of this first Kinslaying weighs for a long time upon relations with the exiled Noldor.</p>\n<h2>War</h2>\n<p>The Teleri do not organise their society around war. When they must fight, they favour mobility, precision, bows, spears, javelins and manageable blades suited to quays and ships. Light protection better suits their environment.</p>\n<h2>Place in character creation</h2>\n<p>Teleri Culture is intended for the First Age. A character should be tied to Alqualondë, Tol Eressëa or the shores of Aman, with a precise date determining their relationship with the Noldor and the Kinslaying at Alqualondë.</p>","culture":"Teleri — Falmari"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":{"1":{"sourceKey":"culture-teleri-age-1","folderKey":"cultures-age-1-elves","document":{"name":"Teleri — Falmari","type":"culture","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"culture-teleri-age-1","canonicalKey":"culture-teleri","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":1,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-conversion","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5"},"raceKey":"race-elves-age-1","raceCanonicalKey":"race-elves","availability":{"age":1,"ageLabel":"Premier Âge","restricted":false},"descriptionFormat":"html","languages":{"starting":[],"source":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5","sourceStatus":"starting-languages-not-resolv